{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    // The tests import each other by explicit ".mts" path ("./scratch.mts"),
    // which is what Node's ESM resolver requires. Without this, every one of
    // those imports is TS5097 -- 134 of the 148 errors that appeared the
    // moment the .mts glob was added, and not one of them a real defect.
    // Safe here because this project never emits from tsc: noEmit is on.
    // NB line comments, not a block: a tsconfig comment containing a glob
    // like "**/" closes a block comment early, because "*/" is inside it.
    // That silently cost the "paths" mapping and produced 1211 errors.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [{ "name": "next" }],
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./*"]
    }
  },
  // The .mts glob is here because it was not, and nobody could see that. (N18)
  //
  // Every test in this repo is .mts -- 21 of 21, zero .ts -- so this list
  // typechecked lib/, app/ and scripts/ and not one line of tests/, for as
  // long as the suite has existed. scripts/bundle-app reads scripts/test's
  // exit code as the release gate, and that run's typecheck was never looking
  // at the tests, so "tsc clean" meant less than everyone quoting it believed.
  //
  // It cost two real fail-opens: the incomplete ClipProbe fixtures in
  // grouping.test.mts and import-batch.test.mts both feed
  // analyseBatch -> joinRefusal, and the typechecker names them in its first
  // two lines.
  //
  // scripts/guard-typecheck.py fails the run if a file under tests/ is
  // outside this set, so the next sibling extension cannot repeat it. It
  // found one on its first run: tests/native/fake-occupant.mjs, an 88-line
  // fake server that N8's launch probe drives and that nothing was checking.
  // Hence the .mjs glob -- allowJs is already on, so the three .mjs files in
  // this repo are parsed rather than type-checked, which is enough to catch a
  // syntax error in a test helper and costs nothing.
  "include": ["next-env.d.ts", "**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx", "**/*.mts", "**/*.mjs", ".next/types/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "ugc-edit-system"]
}
